## Migration: `BackgroundProcessor` → .NET 8

### Patterns Identified

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Cancellable work loop | `while (_running)` | `CancellationToken` |
| ThreadAbortException catch | `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| Thread.ResetAbort | Prevents rethrow to keep thread alive | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |
| Blocking call interruption | `GetNextItem()` may block | Pass token to unblock |
| Thread.Abort() | `Stop()` method | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (migrated from ThreadAbortException handler)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we just continue to loop exit naturally
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes to `GetNextItem`

The blocking `GetNextItem()` **must** accept and honor the token — otherwise cancellation stalls there:

```csharp
// BEFORE: blocks indefinitely, only Thread.Abort could interrupt
private Item GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// AFTER: unblocks when token is cancelled (throws OperationCanceledException)
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
// Works with BlockingCollection<T>.Take(CancellationToken)
```

If `GetNextItem` does synchronous I/O (socket, file), replace with async overloads or use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(timeout)` polling.

---

### Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| Not passing token to `GetNextItem` | Thread stays blocked forever — `Cancel()` has no effect on blocking calls that don't check the token |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` without the `when` filter | You'd swallow unrelated cancellations from inner operations |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks timers and registered callbacks |
| Expecting `Cancel()` to be instant | It only takes effect at the next checkpoint; if `ProcessItem` is long-running, add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside it |
| Using `Thread.Sleep` elsewhere in the class | Replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` so it wakes on cancellation |

---

### Why Thread.Abort Fails on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` at runtime — there is no way to forcibly inject an exception into a running thread. The entire model shifts from **preemptive** (runtime forces stop) to **cooperative** (code checks and responds to cancellation).
